-- allium: 3
-- agent-tree.allium
--
-- Scope: The agent file tree panel — a companion pane living inside each
--        agent's own tmux window that shows which files that agent has read
--        and modified.
-- Includes: The capture pipeline (a PostToolUse-driven, per-task JSONL event
--           log of Read/Write/Edit/NotebookEdit tool calls); the companion
--           pane's lifecycle (split at agent-window creation, global toggle
--           keybinding bound alongside the board TUI, kill-pane + re-split);
--           and the tree/badge rendering rules (worktree-rooted tree, Modified
--           badge beating Read, auto-expanded directories).
-- Excludes: Task-activity classification and the existing activity-signal hook
--           (see agent-health.allium — HookPreToolUse fires for both PreToolUse
--           and PostToolUse and is NOT modified by anything in this spec);
--           MCP tool-call auditing and slow-DB warnings (see
--           observability.allium, which deliberately scopes out hook events);
--           the board TUI's own split pane, which joins a whole agent window
--           next to the kanban board (see split-pane.allium — a different pane,
--           in a different window, with a different lifecycle);
--           agent-window creation itself and the main-session window (see
--           dispatch.allium).
--
-- Two deliberately decoupled halves. CAPTURE is driven by Claude Code hooks and
-- runs whether or not anything is watching: a dispatched agent fills its event
-- log even when the board TUI is not running and no pane is open. RENDER is a
-- standalone process inside the agent's own tmux window that tails that log.
-- Neither half touches the board TUI's state, its DB refresh cycle, or the
-- kanban App — the whole feature is invisible to the board process.

use "./core.allium" as core

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The Claude Code tools whose calls are captured. Every other tool call
-- (including Bash, Grep, Glob, Task and every mcp__dispatch__* call) produces
-- no FileEvent — see the BashGap note on CaptureFileEvent.
enum TrackedFileTool { read | write | edit | notebook_edit }

-- What a captured tool call did to the file. Also the badge vocabulary: a
-- rendered node's badge is a FileOperation, so badge precedence
-- (modified beats read) is a comparison within one named enum rather than
-- across two anonymous ones.
enum FileOperation { read | modified }

enum TreeNodeKind { file | directory }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

-- One line in <dispatch_data_dir>/<file_events_subdir>/<task_id>.jsonl.
-- Every captured tool call becomes one FileEvent, serialised as a single JSON
-- object on its own line. Events for a task accumulate in a single file named
-- by the task's integer ID, under the centralized data dir (parent of the
-- SQLite database) so the log outlives worktree cleanup — the same storage
-- shape observability.allium uses for TrajectoryEntry.
--
-- The file shard coincides exactly with the natural serialization boundary:
-- one task, one agent session, one strictly serial tool-call stream, so at
-- most one process ever appends to a given task's file at a time. That, not
-- precedent, is why an append-only file is safe here.
value FileEvent {
    schema_version: String        -- semver string, e.g. "1.0.0"
    timestamp:      Timestamp     -- UTC, RFC 3339
    task_id:        String        -- stringified Task.id of the agent that tooled
    tool:           TrackedFileTool
    path:           String        -- path exactly as the tool reported it
                                  -- (absolute); relativised at render time
    operation:      FileOperation -- read for read; modified for the other three
}

-- One row of the rendered tree. Nodes are derived, not stored: they are
-- recomputed from the task's FileEvent log on every refresh, so a node
-- carries no identity of its own. A node exists only for a touched path or an
-- ancestor directory of one — an untouched file or directory never gets a
-- node, rather than appearing collapsed (see RefreshAgentTree).
value AgentTreeNode {
    pane_root: String            -- the pane root this node was rendered under
    path:      String            -- path relative to pane_root
    kind:      TreeNodeKind
    badge:     FileOperation?    -- null for directories and for untouched files
    expanded:  Boolean           -- directories only; always false for files
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The companion pane inside one agent's tmux window. Created by splitting that
-- window at creation time; toggled between shown and hidden by killing and
-- re-splitting the pane. Its state is not persisted — it is re-derived from
-- live tmux, the same way dispatch.allium re-derives main-session liveness.
entity AgentTreePane {
    -- The agent window this pane was split out of. One pane per window
    -- (see OneCompanionPanePerAgentWindow).
    window: core/TmuxWindow

    -- The task whose event log feeds this pane. Required: a companion pane
    -- exists only for a task-agent window. The main-session window has no task
    -- and gets no pane at all — see SplitAgentTreePaneOnAgentLaunch's
    -- "Resolves MainSessionPaneScope" note.
    task: core/Task

    -- Absolute directory the tree is rooted at: always the task's worktree path
    -- (see TaskPaneRootIsTaskWorktree).
    root: String

    status: shown | hidden

    -- The tmux pane exists only while the pane is shown; hiding kills it.
    tmux_pane: core/TmuxPane when status = shown

    -- Stamped on every refresh; drives the redraw timer (see RefreshAgentTree).
    last_rendered_at: Timestamp

    -- The editor pane opened from this tree, if any. At most one per agent
    -- window (see OneEditorPanePerAgentWindow): opening a second file replaces
    -- what this pane is running rather than adding another pane. It lives in
    -- the same window as this pane but is NOT an AgentTreePane — nothing
    -- renders a tree in it, and the toggle does not act on it.
    editor_pane: core/TmuxPane?

    -- Which file the editor pane is showing, relative to root. Set on every
    -- open, so it is what distinguishes "replaced the file" from "did nothing"
    -- when the pane is reused (see ReplaceAgentTreeEditorFile).
    editor_file: String? when editor_pane != null

    -- Why the last attempt to open a file failed, shown in this pane's own
    -- border until the next keypress. The only thing the pane ever reports to
    -- the user (see AgentTreeEditorOpenFailureIsVisible).
    error_notice: String?

    transitions status {
        shown -> hidden
        hidden -> shown
    }
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    file_event_schema_version: String = "1.0.0"

    -- Subdirectory within the dispatch data dir that holds per-task file-event
    -- logs. Sibling of observability.allium's trajectory_subdir.
    file_events_subdir: String = "file-events"

    -- Width of the companion pane as a percentage of the agent window.
    -- Deliberately narrower than the board's own 40% split-pane: Claude's CLI
    -- output needs the room, and a file tree is mostly short path segments.
    agent_tree_pane_percent: Integer = 30

    -- Redraw cadence. The renderer re-reads an append-only file it already has
    -- open, so a one-second poll is cheap; it is also well below the point
    -- where a follow-along view feels laggy. Not tied to the board TUI's
    -- tick_interval — the two processes are independent.
    agent_tree_refresh_interval: Duration = 1.second

    -- The tmux key (pressed after the tmux prefix) that toggles the companion
    -- pane. "e" for explorer: unbound in tmux's default key table, so the
    -- binding shadows nothing. See the ToggleKeyChoice open question.
    agent_tree_toggle_key: String = "e"

    -- Height of the editor pane as a percentage of the agent window. It spans
    -- the full window WIDTH, so the tree and the agent's own pane share the
    -- remaining 40% above it — unlike agent_tree_pane_percent, which divides
    -- the window horizontally. Deliberately the larger share: reading the file
    -- is the point of opening it.
    agent_tree_editor_pane_percent: Integer = 60

    -- Which editor to launch is NOT declared here: it is core/config.editor_fallback
    -- and the resolution rule stated with it, shared with the pop-out task and
    -- epic editors (see OpenAgentTreeFileInEditor's guidance).
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- == Capture ==

rule CaptureFileEvent {
    -- After a dispatched agent completes a file-touching tool call, append one
    -- FileEvent to that task's event log.
    when: AgentFileToolCompleted(task, tool, path)

    -- A tool call that reported no file path (a malformed payload, or a tool
    -- schema change that renamed the path field) is skipped, not captured and
    -- not an error. Soft-fail: the agent's tool call must not be disturbed by
    -- anything this rule does.
    requires: path != ""

    ensures: append_jsonl(
        path: dispatch_data_dir
              + "/" + config.file_events_subdir
              + "/" + str(task.id) + ".jsonl",
        entry: FileEvent {
            schema_version: config.file_event_schema_version,
            timestamp: now,
            task_id: str(task.id),
            tool: tool,
            path: path,
            operation: if tool = read: read else: modified
        }
    )

    @guidance
        -- == Where the trigger comes from ==
        -- AgentFileToolCompleted is emitted by the PostToolUse half of the
        -- shell hook's existing PreToolUse|PostToolUse case arm, only for the
        -- four tracked tools, and only when a path was extracted from the
        -- payload.
        --
        -- ADDITIVE, NOT A REPLACEMENT. The same case arm's existing
        -- `dispatch hook <id> pre_tool_use` call is untouched: it still fires
        -- on BOTH events and for ALL tools (minus the mcp__dispatch__* early
        -- exit), which is what keeps agent-health.allium's HookPreToolUse
        -- stamping last_pre_tool_use_at. This rule adds a second, independent
        -- call on the PostToolUse half; it does not gate, reorder or replace
        -- the activity signal, and it introduces no new HookEventKind variant.
        -- record_hook_event never sees a tool name or a file path — that enum
        -- and that service path stay narrowly scoped to task-activity/
        -- sub-status classification, and this capture deliberately routes
        -- around them through its own CLI subcommand.
        --
        -- == Per-tool path field ==
        -- Read, Write and Edit report the path as tool_input.file_path;
        -- NotebookEdit reports it as tool_input.notebook_path. The mapping is
        -- per-tool and must be revisited if Claude Code changes tool schemas —
        -- a rename shows up as a silently empty path, which this rule's
        -- requires clause turns into "no event" rather than a crash.
        --
        -- == The Bash gap ==
        -- Files touched only through Bash (mv, sed -i, redirects) carry no
        -- structured path in the payload and are therefore invisible to the
        -- tree. Mitigated outside this spec by a global agent rule file that
        -- steers agents toward Read/Write/Edit over raw shell file operations.
        -- A blocking PreToolUse hook is the documented fallback if that proves
        -- insufficient; it is out of scope here.
        --
        -- == Success is assumed ==
        -- Whether the PostToolUse payload reliably signals a FAILED tool call
        -- (an Edit whose old_string did not match, a Write denied by
        -- permissions) is unconfirmed. Every captured PostToolUse is therefore
        -- treated as a successful touch. Accepted inaccuracy: a file can carry
        -- a Modified badge on the strength of an edit that did not land.
        --
        -- == Independent of the board ==
        -- Capture runs in the agent's own short-lived hook process. It needs no
        -- board TUI, no companion pane and no MCP connection, so an agent
        -- dispatched with the board closed still builds a complete log, and a
        -- pane opened later shows the whole history from the first tool call.
}

rule FileEventWriteFailureIsSilent {
    -- Event-log writes are best-effort. File I/O errors (permission denied,
    -- disk full) must not propagate into the agent's tool call.
    when: FileEventWriteFailed(task_id, error)

    ensures: FileEventWriteWarning(task_id: task_id, error: error)

    @guidance
        -- Mirrors observability.allium's TrajectoryWriteFailureIsSilent: the
        -- append is wrapped in a fallible block, the error goes to a warn-level
        -- log, and the hook process exits successfully anyway. No retry, no
        -- buffering, no dead-letter file. A dropped event costs one missing
        -- badge, which is not worth failing an agent's tool call over.
}

-- == Companion Pane Lifecycle ==
--
-- The toggle is a GLOBAL tmux keybinding, so like split-pane.allium's
-- prefix+space jump-back binding its lifetime is the board TUI process's
-- lifetime: bound when the board starts, unbound when it exits. A companion
-- pane created by an agent window survives the board's exit — only the ability
-- to toggle it goes away.

rule BindAgentTreeToggle {
    when: AppStarts()

    ensures: AgentTreeToggleBound(key: config.agent_tree_toggle_key)

    @guidance
        -- Registered as a prefixed tmux binding alongside the existing
        -- prefix+space jump-back binding (see split-pane.allium:
        -- JumpBackToTuiWindow), through the same bind_key helper and the same
        -- board-startup path. Best-effort: a failing tmux call must not
        -- prevent the board TUI from starting.
        --
        -- == IMPLEMENTED ==
        -- setup_tmux_for_tui (src/runtime/mod.rs) binds
        -- config.agent_tree_toggle_key right after the existing prefix+space
        -- bind_key call, same function, same best-effort `let _ =` style.
        --
        -- The bound command is a new pattern for this codebase: a
        -- format-string-parameterized run-shell, not a static tmux command
        -- like prefix+space's `select-window -t =TUI`. tmux itself expands
        -- #{window_name} into the command line at the moment the key is
        -- pressed, before invoking the shell — so the process this spawns
        -- (`dispatch toggle-agent-tree-pane <window>`) is handed the name of
        -- whichever window was focused, without having to ask tmux itself.
        -- The `-b` flag backgrounds the shell job so the keypress does not
        -- block the tmux client while it runs. See ToggleAgentTreePane's own
        -- guidance for what that process does with the window name.
        --
        -- Resolves ToggleKeyChoice: config.agent_tree_toggle_key's default
        -- ("e") was adopted as-is, unchanged from the design's own rationale
        -- (unbound in tmux's default key table, no collision with
        -- prefix+space).
        --
        -- Surfaced in the in-TUI help popup ([?], General section) alongside
        -- the jump-back binding — otherwise nothing in the TUI hints that a
        -- companion pane can be toggled at all.
}

rule UnbindAgentTreeToggle {
    when: AppExits()

    ensures: AgentTreeToggleUnbound(key: config.agent_tree_toggle_key)

    @guidance
        -- Torn down with the rest of the board's tmux setup, so the key is
        -- returned to whatever tmux had bound it to. Consequence, shared with
        -- prefix+space: the companion pane cannot be toggled while the board
        -- TUI is not running.
        --
        -- == IMPLEMENTED ==
        -- teardown_tmux_for_tui (src/runtime/mod.rs) unbinds
        -- config.agent_tree_toggle_key alongside the existing prefix+space
        -- unbind_key call.
}

rule SplitAgentTreePaneOnAgentLaunch {
    -- Every agent window gets a companion pane the moment it is created, and
    -- only an agent window does. One rule covers every launch path because they
    -- all emit AgentLaunched: fresh dispatch, research dispatch, quick dispatch,
    -- epic auto-dispatch, resume and retry-resume.
    when: AgentLaunched(task, mode)

    requires: task.tmux_window != null
    requires: task.worktree != null

    ensures: AgentTreePane.created(
        window: task.tmux_window,
        task: task,
        root: task.worktree.path,
        status: shown,
        tmux_pane: core/TmuxPane.created(),
        last_rendered_at: now
    )

    @guidance
        -- The window is split immediately after it is created and the agent
        -- command has been sent, using a split helper that takes both a size
        -- (config.agent_tree_pane_percent) and a command to run in the new
        -- pane — the same "create and immediately run a command" shape the
        -- window-creation helper already establishes. The board's existing
        -- 40%-hardcoded horizontal split helper is left alone; it serves the
        -- board's own split-pane feature (see split-pane.allium).
        --
        -- The new pane runs a standalone tree renderer for this task
        -- (`dispatch agent-tree <task_id>`), not part of the board's App. It
        -- opens even when the task's log is still empty, in which case the tree
        -- it shows is empty too: only touched paths and their ancestors are
        -- rendered, so a pane opened before the agent's first file tool call
        -- shows nothing but the root (see RefreshAgentTree).
        --
        -- NO RETROFIT. Creation is triggered only by window creation, so agent
        -- windows that already existed when this feature shipped never grow a
        -- pane on their own automatically. A manual toggle can still create
        -- one on demand in such a window — see ShowAgentTreePane's
        -- "Resolves ToggleTargetResolution" note, which is a different
        -- trigger (a deliberate keypress) than the automatic creation this
        -- rule covers.
        --
        -- == IMPLEMENTED ==
        -- The size-and-command split helper described above is
        -- tmux::split_window_horizontal_running (src/tmux.rs), a sibling of the
        -- board's 40%-hardcoded split_window_horizontal, which is untouched.
        -- It passes the task's worktree as the new pane's start directory
        -- (`split-window -c`), which is what keeps the companion pane out of the
        -- after-split-window correction hook — see split-pane.allium's
        -- AgentWindowSplitStartsInTaskWorktree. The toggle and resync paths hold
        -- only a window name, so they read the worktree back from that window's
        -- @dispatch_dir option.
        -- spawn_agent_tree_pane (src/dispatch/agents.rs) calls it at
        -- config.agent_tree_pane_percent and is wired into dispatch_with_prompt
        -- — covering fresh dispatch, research dispatch and quick dispatch, and
        -- through them epic auto-dispatch — and into resume_agent, covering
        -- resume and retry-resume. In both cases the call sits immediately
        -- after the existing send-keys of the agent command, which is the
        -- "after the agent command has been sent" timing above.
        --
        -- The new pane opens on the LEFT of the agent's own pane (tmux
        -- split-window's -b/"before" flag), keeping focus (-d) on the agent's
        -- pane to its right. Unlike the board's own split_window_horizontal
        -- (no -b, right pane, used by split-pane.allium), the tree reads
        -- left-to-right the way a file tree conventionally does, with the
        -- agent's CLI output remaining the wider, focused pane.
        --
        -- Soft-fail, deliberately: a split that fails is logged at warn level
        -- and the dispatch or resume call still succeeds. No agent fails to
        -- launch because its companion pane could not open — consistent with
        -- BindAgentTreeToggle's best-effort binding.
        --
        -- Resolves MainSessionPaneScope: the main-session window is EXCLUDED
        -- from the split, so AgentLaunched is the only trigger that ever creates
        -- a companion pane, and AgentTreePane.task is required rather than
        -- optional. The main session has no task and no worktree, so its pane
        -- would render an always-empty tree forever: the capture hook derives its
        -- task id from the worktree's numeric branch prefix and would exit
        -- without capturing anything, and `dispatch agent-tree` takes a REQUIRED
        -- task id (src/cli/agent_tree.rs) so there is no id to launch it with.
        -- Such a pane would also be covered by neither pane-teardown rule below,
        -- since both key off a Task. Consistency across the three
        -- window-creation paths was the original motivation and is not worth a
        -- permanently empty pane that nothing can tear down.
        --
        -- The alternative the question also offered — teaching capture to
        -- attribute main-session tool calls to a non-task log — is not merely
        -- deferred but rejected: it would require a second log identity, a
        -- renderer that accepts no task, and teardown rules keyed off something
        -- other than a Task, to earn a tree of a directory the user is already
        -- looking at.
        --
        -- Asserted in tests/tmux_lifecycle.rs
        -- (main_session_window_has_a_single_pane), so the absence is covered
        -- rather than merely unimplemented.
}

rule HideAgentTreePane {
    when: ToggleAgentTreePane(user)

    let pane = AgentTreePane{window: current_tmux_window()}

    requires: exists pane
    requires: pane.status = shown

    ensures: pane.status = hidden
    ensures: pane.tmux_pane = null

    @guidance
        -- Hiding kills the pane outright rather than zooming or resizing it,
        -- matching the board's own enter/exit split mechanism (kill-pane, then
        -- re-split on the way back). The cost is that the renderer process dies
        -- and restarts on the next show; that is cheap, because all its state
        -- comes from re-reading the append-only event log.
        --
        -- == The editor pane is NOT hidden with it ==
        -- Only the tree pane is killed. An editor pane opened from it stays
        -- exactly where it was, still showing its file: the user asked for that
        -- file, and did not ask for it to close when they collapsed the tree.
        -- Nothing is orphaned by this, because the pane is found by a mark on
        -- the pane itself rather than by anything the renderer holds — so a
        -- later show, in a new renderer process, still reuses that same pane on
        -- the next open (see ReplaceAgentTreeEditorFile). The pane's own life
        -- ends with the editor running in it, or with the window
        -- (DiscardAgentTreePaneOnWindowGone), or when the window is pinned into
        -- the board (see the ToggleVsSplitPaneInteraction note below).
        --
        -- The killed pane is the companion pane, never the agent's own pane:
        -- the target is resolved from the AgentTreePane record for the focused
        -- window, not from "whichever pane is not focused".
        --
        -- == IMPLEMENTED ==
        -- No AgentTreePane record is persisted anywhere (no DB row, no tmux
        -- user option) — `let pane = AgentTreePane{window: current_tmux_window()}`
        -- above is a spec-level abstraction, not a stored lookup. The
        -- implementation re-derives both halves of it live from tmux on every
        -- toggle: `current_tmux_window()` is resolved by tmux's own
        -- #{window_name} format-string expansion inside the bound run-shell
        -- command (see BindAgentTreeToggle), before dispatch even starts —
        -- this process is simply handed the window name as an argv string.
        -- `pane.status = shown` means the window HAS a companion pane, which is
        -- read by identifying that pane directly (below), not from any stored
        -- status field.
        --
        -- == How the companion pane is identified ==
        -- By a role marker dispatch writes on the pane at the moment it creates
        -- it: a pane-scoped tmux user option whose value says what the pane is
        -- for. The companion tree pane carries the agent-tree role; the editor
        -- pane OpenAgentTreeFileInEditor opens carries the editor role. The
        -- lookup matches the role's EXACT value, so the two never stand in for
        -- each other — a toggle cannot kill the editor, and an open cannot
        -- respawn the tree. No pane in the window carrying the agent-tree role
        -- means hidden.
        --
        -- One marker, both panes, and that is the point: "a pane dispatch put in
        -- this window" is now exactly "a pane carrying this option", whatever
        -- its role — which is what the pin drain reads (see
        -- ToggleVsSplitPaneInteraction below and split-pane.allium's
        -- PinTaskInSplitPane), in one lookup, and without being taught about any
        -- future kind of dispatch-created pane.
        --
        -- == What this replaced, part 1: the start command ==
        -- Identity was first derived from the command the pane was started with
        -- (tmux's #{pane_start_command}), matched as argv0's basename plus argv1.
        -- It was correct, and it was chosen because it needed no migration — but
        -- it re-derived identity from a string tmux was never asked to keep
        -- stable. It whitespace-split a command line, so a dispatch binary at a
        -- path containing a space mis-parsed; it compared argv0 by basename
        -- because that path may be absolute; and it had to actively defend
        -- against substring matching, because an editor pane showing this very
        -- file has "agent-tree" in its command line. A marker written by the code
        -- that created the pane needs none of those defences: it cannot be
        -- confused by the contents of a file an editor happens to be showing.
        --
        -- ACCEPTED COST: the marker is NOT retroactive. The start command was
        -- reported for panes already running; an option can only be written to a
        -- pane being created. So agent windows already open when this changed
        -- carry unmarked panes, and for the remaining life of each such window
        -- the toggle reads its tree as hidden and splits a SECOND tree pane, and
        -- pinning or resyncing no longer finds those panes. This was accepted
        -- deliberately (#3902): the regression is one-off and self-clearing as
        -- agent windows turn over, and each affected window has its own escape
        -- hatch — the second toggle press kills the marked pane the first one
        -- created, and every pane in a fresh window is marked. The two
        -- alternatives were rejected for the same reason: both keep the string
        -- matcher alive, either as a fallback nothing forces anyone to delete, or
        -- inside new startup code that backfills markers — which moves the
        -- fragility rather than removing it.
        --
        -- == What this replaced, part 2: focus ==
        -- Before either form of marker, the kill target used to be "the window's single INACTIVE pane"
        -- (`tmux::inactive_pane_id`), justified by every split helper passing
        -- `-d`, which leaves focus on the agent's own pane at split time. That
        -- is a property of the split, not of the window, and it failed in two
        -- ways once the user was involved:
        --   * With focus in the companion pane — which the user must take to
        --     press any key in it — the single inactive pane is the AGENT'S, so
        --     the toggle killed the live claude session.
        --   * With an editor pane open (OpenAgentTreeFileInEditor) two panes
        --     are inactive, which read as "hidden", so the toggle split a
        --     SECOND companion pane.
        -- Identifying the pane by what it is rather than by where focus happens
        -- to be is what makes "the companion pane, never the agent's own pane"
        -- true rather than merely usually true. An index-based target remains
        -- wrong for the separate reason it always was: `pane-base-index` shifts
        -- indices and a `-b` split renumbers them.
        --
        -- == RESOLVED: ToggleVsSplitPaneInteraction ==
        -- The former open question here asked whether the board's
        -- split-pane join/swap operations (split-pane.allium: `join_pane`,
        -- `exec_swap_split_pane`) needed to become companion-pane-aware,
        -- since both predate every agent window carrying a companion pane.
        -- Both sides needed fixing, and the first answer recorded here got the
        -- division wrong. On the mutation side: `join_pane` drains the
        -- companion panes left behind when it moves the agent's own pane out
        -- (see split-pane.allium's PinTaskInSplitPane guidance) — every pane
        -- dispatch put in that window, the editor pane included, since with one
        -- open the old single-inactive-pane lookup was ambiguous and orphaned
        -- both. `exec_swap_split_pane` resyncs a renamed window's companion
        -- pane to the task the new name implies (see SwapSplitPane's guidance).
        -- But this rule's own state-reading was NOT "already correct" as
        -- previously claimed: see "What this replaced" above.
        --
        -- A window name that doesn't parse as `task-<id>` (the inverse of
        -- build_tmux_window_name — the board's own TUI window, the
        -- unimplemented main-session window, anything else) never reaches the
        -- `exists pane` check at all: toggle_agent_tree_pane
        -- (src/dispatch/agents.rs) short-circuits to a no-op before issuing
        -- any tmux call, which stands in for "not exists pane" without a
        -- record to test.
}

rule ShowAgentTreePane {
    when: ToggleAgentTreePane(user)

    let pane = AgentTreePane{window: current_tmux_window()}

    requires: exists pane
    requires: pane.status = hidden

    ensures: pane.status = shown
    ensures: pane.tmux_pane = core/TmuxPane.created()
    ensures: pane.last_rendered_at = now

    @guidance
        -- Re-splits the window at config.agent_tree_pane_percent and restarts
        -- the renderer, which rebuilds the full tree from the event log — so a
        -- hide/show cycle loses no history, only the scroll position and any
        -- manual expand/collapse the user had done.
        --
        -- == IMPLEMENTED ==
        -- `pane.status = hidden` above is read the same live way
        -- HideAgentTreePane's guidance describes: no pane in the window carries
        -- the agent-tree role marker. The re-split reuses
        -- SplitAgentTreePaneOnAgentLaunch's own
        -- implementation directly — toggle_agent_tree_pane
        -- (src/dispatch/agents.rs) calls the same spawn_agent_tree_pane
        -- helper that agent-launch time uses, so the show side of the toggle
        -- and the initial split are one code path, not two. The task id it
        -- passes to `dispatch agent-tree <task_id>` comes from parsing the
        -- window name (`task-<id>`), never from a stored pane record.
        --
        -- Resolves ToggleTargetResolution in favour of retrofitting: because
        -- "hidden" and "never had a pane" are the same live tmux state (no pane
        -- carrying the marker), a task-<id> window that predates this feature —
        -- or one whose automatic split at launch failed, or one whose pane
        -- predates the role marker itself (see HideAgentTreePane's ACCEPTED
        -- COST) — gets a marked companion pane the first time a user manually
        -- toggles it. That is also what bounds that accepted regression: the
        -- unmarked pane survives one extra press, and every pane the toggle
        -- itself creates is marked. This does not violate
        -- SplitAgentTreePaneOnAgentLaunch's "no retrofit" guidance, which is
        -- about automatic creation on launch; a deliberate user keypress is a
        -- different trigger.
}

rule DiscardAgentTreePaneOnWindowGone {
    -- The companion pane lives inside the agent window. When that window dies,
    -- the pane dies with it, whether or not dispatch asked for it.
    when: WindowGone(task)

    let pane = AgentTreePane{task: task}

    requires: exists pane

    ensures: not exists pane

    @guidance
        -- Covers the crash path specifically: agent-health.allium's
        -- DetectCrashedAgent deliberately leaves task.tmux_window set so the
        -- retry prompt can still name the window, so the field-transition rule
        -- below would never fire for a crashed agent. The pane record must go
        -- anyway — the tmux pane it refers to no longer exists.
}

rule DiscardAgentTreePaneOnWindowCleared {
    -- Covers every lifecycle path that clears a task's window: wrap-up,
    -- PR merge, manual Review -> Done, archive, and the kill half of a
    -- retry-resume.
    when: task: core/Task.tmux_window transitions_to null

    let pane = AgentTreePane{task: task}

    requires: exists pane

    ensures: not exists pane

    @guidance
        -- Mirrors the trigger split-pane.allium's
        -- SplitPaneRespawnOnWindowCleared uses, for the same reason: keying on
        -- the field transition rather than on each individual lifecycle event
        -- means no path can be forgotten. Unlike the board's split pane, there
        -- is nothing to respawn — the pane is inside the window being
        -- destroyed, so it is simply gone. A retry-resume produces a fresh
        -- pane via SplitAgentTreePaneOnAgentLaunch.
}

-- == Rendering ==

rule RefreshAgentTree {
    -- A shown pane redraws on a timer, picking up whatever lines have landed in
    -- the event log since the last pass.
    when: pane: AgentTreePane.last_rendered_at + config.agent_tree_refresh_interval <= now

    -- Hidden panes have no renderer process. The timestamp is not advanced
    -- while hidden, so the trigger stays satisfied and the first refresh
    -- happens immediately on show.
    requires: pane.status = shown

    -- Events for this pane's task, restricted to paths that lie under the
    -- pane's root. A tool call against a file outside the worktree (a global
    -- config file, a sibling repo) is still captured in the log, but has no
    -- node in a tree rooted at the worktree and so contributes nothing here.
    let events = file_events_under(file_events_for(pane.task), pane.root)

    ensures: pane.last_rendered_at = now
    ensures:
        for entry in touched_tree(events):
            AgentTreeNodeRendered(
                pane: pane,
                node: AgentTreeNode {
                    pane_root: pane.root,
                    path: entry.path,
                    kind: entry.kind,
                    -- Badge precedence: a file that was both read and modified
                    -- shows Modified. Directories are never badged.
                    badge:
                        if entry.kind = directory: null
                        else if events_for_path(events, entry.path).any(e => e.operation = modified): modified
                        else if events_for_path(events, entry.path).count > 0: read
                        else: null,
                    -- Auto-expand: a directory opens exactly when something
                    -- under it was touched, so the path to every badge is
                    -- visible without the view degenerating into a full repo
                    -- browser.
                    expanded: entry.kind = directory
                              and has_touched_descendant(events, entry.path)
                }
            )

    @guidance
        -- == The three rendering rules, in one place ==
        --   1. ROOT. The tree is rooted at the pane's root — the task's
        --      worktree, never the parent repo. Node paths are relative to it;
        --      the log stores absolute paths, so relativisation happens here.
        --      A node exists only for a touched path or an ancestor directory
        --      of one; an untouched sibling never gets a node at all, so the
        --      tree is a projection of the event log, not the filesystem.
        --   2. BADGES. Write, Edit and NotebookEdit produce Modified; Read
        --      produces Read; a file with both shows Modified. Modified wins
        --      because "this agent changed this file" is the fact the user is
        --      scanning for, and it is strictly the more consequential of the
        --      two.
        --   3. EXPANSION. Every directory node is, by construction, an
        --      ancestor of a touched path, so every directory that appears is
        --      expanded — there is no untouched, collapsed directory to
        --      reveal. A manual collapse is not overwritten by the next
        --      refresh; only newly-touched paths add new nodes.
        --
        -- == Black boxes ==
        -- touched_tree(events) derives (path, kind) entries for every distinct
        -- touched path and each of its ancestor directories, deduplicated.
        -- events_for_path / has_touched_descendant are pure lookups over the
        -- already-loaded event set. A malformed line in the log is skipped
        -- rather than aborting the refresh, so one bad append cannot blank the
        -- tree.
        --
        -- == NOT IMPLEMENTED — DEFERRED: the worktree_tree traversal ==
        -- Scoped to the traversal only. The refresh cadence, the file-stamp
        -- short-circuit, badge precedence and auto-expansion all ship as
        -- written; what does not ship is walking the worktree.
        --
        -- The shipped renderer performs NO filesystem traversal at all. Its
        -- tree is built purely from the event log (build_tree in
        -- src/agent_tree.rs, called by src/cli/agent_tree.rs), so it contains
        -- exactly the touched paths plus the ancestor directories needed to
        -- reach them. Untouched siblings are absent, not collapsed. Two
        -- consequences follow, and they are the shipped behaviour:
        --   * An empty event log renders an EMPTY tree, not the worktree with
        --     nothing badged.
        --   * The tree is not a repo browser the user can open into. There is
        --     nothing under an untouched directory to expand, because untouched
        --     directories are not rendered.
        --
        -- Merging the event-derived tree with a real directory listing was
        -- deliberately left to a follow-up, and TreeScanExclusions is the
        -- question that gates it: an unfiltered eager scan of a large repo is
        -- both slow and mostly noise. Until it is answered, a touched-paths-only
        -- tree is the honest description of what the pane shows.
        --
        -- == Implementation notes ==
        -- The renderer is a small standalone ratatui loop reusing the existing
        -- tree widget dependency; it is not part of the board's App and shares
        -- none of its state.
        --
        -- == Auto-expansion must be verified by RENDERING ==
        -- The EXPANSION clause above has to be covered by rendering a deeply
        -- nested touched path and asserting the LEAF is on screen — not by
        -- inspecting the tree widget's open-set. Asking the widget to open a
        -- directory reports success even when the key identifies no node, so
        -- an open-set assertion can encode the wrong key and pass while every
        -- nested directory renders collapsed. That was the shipped behaviour
        -- until #3811, under a green open-set test.
}

-- == Companion Pane Input ==
--
-- Keys pressed inside the companion pane are handled by the renderer process
-- itself, with no tmux prefix and no involvement from the board TUI. Only the
-- exit key has a consequence outside the renderer's own view, so only it gets a
-- rule; the cursor and expansion keys are specified on the
-- AgentTreeCompanionPane surface (see the AgentTreeViewState open question).

rule ExitAgentTreeRenderer {
    -- q, or Ctrl-C, stops the renderer. tmux closes a pane when the command
    -- running in it exits, so the companion pane goes with it. The pane is
    -- bound by the trigger rather than looked up: the keystroke arrived inside
    -- one specific renderer, which knows which pane it is running in.
    when: ExitAgentTreeRenderer(user, pane)

    requires: pane.status = shown

    ensures: pane.status = hidden
    ensures: pane.tmux_pane = null

    @guidance
        -- Reaches the same hidden state HideAgentTreePane describes, by a
        -- different route: the renderer returns from its event loop rather than
        -- the board issuing kill-pane. Nothing is lost either way, since the
        -- whole tree is re-derived from the append-only event log.
        --
        -- Capture is unaffected. The agent keeps appending FileEvents with no
        -- pane open, so a renderer started later shows the full history — which
        -- is the point of decoupling the two halves. But because
        -- ShowAgentTreePane is deferred and no toggle key is bound, there is
        -- today no way to start one: exiting the renderer closes the tree for the
        -- remaining life of that agent window.
}

rule OpenAgentTreeFileInEditor {
    -- Space or Enter on a file node, in a window that has no editor pane yet:
    -- open one below the tree showing that file.
    when: OpenSelectedAgentTreeFile(user, pane, node)

    requires: node.kind = file
    requires: pane.editor_pane = null
    -- The agent may have deleted the file since touching it. The event log is
    -- append-only and records no deletions, so a node can outlive its file.
    requires: file_exists(pane.root + "/" + node.path)

    ensures: pane.editor_pane = core/TmuxPane.created()
    ensures: pane.editor_file = node.path
    ensures: pane.error_notice = null

    @guidance
        -- == Geometry ==
        -- The pane spans the full window WIDTH and takes
        -- config.agent_tree_editor_pane_percent of its height, below both the
        -- tree and the agent's own pane. Spanning the window rather than
        -- subdividing the pane it was split from is what makes the geometry
        -- independent of which pane the split targeted — the tree pane is the
        -- natural target and it is the narrow one.
        --
        -- == Focus does not move ==
        -- The interaction this is designed for is browsing: move the cursor,
        -- open a file, move on, open the next. So the keystroke that opens a
        -- file leaves tmux focus in the tree pane, and reaching the editor to
        -- type in it is a deliberate second act (the tmux prefix plus a
        -- direction). This is the one place this module's pane behaviour differs
        -- in KIND from the board's split pane rather than in degree.
        --
        -- == Which editor ==
        -- Resolved by the shared rule stated with core/config.editor_fallback:
        -- VISUAL, else EDITOR, else that fallback; a value that is absent,
        -- empty or only whitespace counts as unset; the value is split into
        -- argv and executed directly with no shell. This surface adds nothing
        -- to that rule — it is the same resolution the pop-out task and epic
        -- editors use, so one $EDITOR means one thing everywhere.
        --
        -- What IS specific here: the file is passed as an absolute path and the
        -- pane starts in pane.root, so a relative path would resolve anyway;
        -- absolute is what makes that not matter.
        --
        -- A GUI editor that forks on launch returns immediately, so its pane
        -- closes while its window stays open. That is the correct outcome and
        -- needs no special handling.
        --
        -- == Not a violation of ReadOnlyObservation ==
        -- The renderer still writes nothing. It launches a process that CAN
        -- write, at the user's explicit keypress — see the amended guarantee on
        -- AgentTreeCompanionPane.
}

rule ReplaceAgentTreeEditorFile {
    -- Space or Enter on a file node when an editor pane is already open: show
    -- the new file THERE rather than opening a second pane.
    when: OpenSelectedAgentTreeFile(user, pane, node)

    requires: node.kind = file
    requires: pane.editor_pane != null
    requires: file_exists(pane.root + "/" + node.path)

    -- The pane itself survives, so there is no new TmuxPane to create and
    -- nothing about the window's layout changes — only which file it shows.
    ensures: pane.editor_file = node.path
    ensures: pane.error_notice = null

    @guidance
        -- One editor pane per window, replaced in place. The alternative —
        -- a pane per opened file — subdivides the window on every press and
        -- leaves the user to clean up, which is the opposite of the browsing
        -- interaction OpenAgentTreeFileInEditor is shaped around.
        --
        -- ACCEPTED COST: replacing kills whatever the pane was running, an
        -- editor with unsaved changes included. This was chosen deliberately
        -- over the two alternatives (stack a new pane; refuse and merely focus
        -- the existing one) in exchange for a layout that does not degrade as
        -- the user browses. It is a real hazard, not an oversight: a user
        -- editing in that pane must move the buffer elsewhere before opening
        -- another file from the tree.
        --
        -- Focus does not move here either, for the same reason as the open
        -- rule — so a user who IS typing in the editor pane cannot trigger this
        -- without first leaving it.
}

rule AgentTreeEditorOpenFailureIsVisible {
    -- Opening a file can fail for reasons the user can neither see nor predict
    -- from the tree: the file is gone, or tmux refused. Say so in the pane.
    when: AgentTreeEditorOpenFailed(pane, reason)

    ensures: pane.error_notice = reason
    ensures: pane.status = shown

    @guidance
        -- Deliberately the OPPOSITE of FileEventWriteFailureIsSilent, and the
        -- difference is who is waiting. A dropped file-event is a background
        -- write nobody asked for, so it costs one badge and a log line. This is
        -- the direct response to a keypress: a silent no-op is indistinguishable
        -- from a broken key, and the user would press it again.
        --
        -- The notice names the reason and nothing else — there is one line of
        -- border to say it in. It is also logged, because the pane's copy is
        -- gone by the next keypress (see ClearAgentTreeErrorNotice).
        --
        -- The renderer keeps running: an editor that cannot be opened must not
        -- take the tree with it. pane.status = shown above is that guarantee,
        -- not a state change.
        --
        -- Covers four failures with one rule because they are one event to the
        -- user: the file no longer exists (the log records touches, never
        -- deletions, so a node outlives its file), the selected path does not lie
        -- inside pane.root, the renderer cannot identify the pane it is running
        -- in (it was started outside tmux), and the tmux split or replace itself
        -- failed.
        --
        -- The out-of-root case is unreachable through the surface as specified —
        -- every node's path is built from segments below the root — and is
        -- rejected anyway rather than trusted to stay that way, since the pane's
        -- rooting at the worktree (TaskPaneRootIsTaskWorktree) is the whole
        -- reason a path is relative in the first place.
}

rule ClearAgentTreeErrorNotice {
    -- Any key the renderer handles acknowledges a notice. There is no separate
    -- dismiss key and no timer.
    when: AgentTreeKeyPressed(pane)

    requires: pane.error_notice != null

    ensures: pane.error_notice = null

    @guidance
        -- The next keypress is the earliest moment the user has demonstrably
        -- seen the notice, which makes it the right thing to clear on: a timer
        -- could expire before they looked, and a dedicated dismiss key would be
        -- one more binding to document for a one-line message.
        --
        -- AgentTreeKeyPressed covers every key on the AgentTreeCompanionPane
        -- surface, the exit key included — clearing state on the way out is
        -- harmless, and the alternative is enumerating exceptions.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------
--
-- The node-scoped black boxes below (events_for_node,
-- has_touched_descendant_node) are the node-only counterparts of
-- RefreshAgentTree's event-scoped ones (events_for_path,
-- has_touched_descendant): an invariant is handed a rendered node, not the
-- event set the refresh pass had in scope, so it re-derives the events for
-- that node from its pane_root and path.

-- Every persisted event must carry the current schema version; readers
-- dispatch on it, so an event without one is unreadable.
invariant FileEventHasSchemaVersion {
    for e in FileEvents:
        e.schema_version != ""
}

-- A captured event always names a file. CaptureFileEvent guards on this, so it
-- holds by construction.
invariant FileEventHasPath {
    for e in FileEvents:
        e.path != ""
}

-- operation is a projection of tool, not an independent field: Read maps to
-- read, and each of the three editing tools maps to modified. Nothing may
-- record a read-only tool as a modification, or vice versa.
invariant FileEventOperationMatchesTool {
    for e in FileEvents:
        (e.tool = read implies e.operation = read)
        and (e.tool != read implies e.operation = modified)
}

-- An event's task_id must identify a real task. Events only ever come from a
-- hook that resolved the id from a live worktree.
invariant FileEventReferencesRealTask {
    for e in FileEvents:
        core/Task.exists(e.task_id)
}

-- One companion pane per agent window. A second split in the same window would
-- steal room from the agent's own pane and leave two renderers tailing the same
-- log.
invariant OneCompanionPanePerAgentWindow {
    for a in AgentTreePanes:
        for b in AgentTreePanes:
            a != b implies a.window != b.window
}

-- At most one editor pane per agent window, and never shared between windows.
-- A second would divide the window again on every open and leave the older one
-- unowned: nothing renders it, the toggle does not act on it, and only the pane
-- that opened it knows it is there.
--
-- Held by construction rather than enforced: an open reuses the pane it finds,
-- and finds it by a mark written immediately after the split — the editor value
-- of the same role marker the toggle reads (see HideAgentTreePane's "How the
-- companion pane is identified"), so one open cannot find the other pane. One
-- gap follows
-- from that and is accepted — if the mark cannot be written, the pane is already
-- open showing the file, so the open reports success and the NEXT open, finding
-- no mark, splits a second pane. Failing the open instead would report a
-- failure that did not happen, over a bookkeeping write.
invariant OneEditorPanePerAgentWindow {
    for a in AgentTreePanes:
        for b in AgentTreePanes:
            a != b implies (a.editor_pane = null or a.editor_pane != b.editor_pane)
}

-- The editor pane and the file it shows exist together or not at all: a pane
-- showing nothing is unreachable (every rule that opens one names a file), and
-- a remembered file with no pane would make ReplaceAgentTreeEditorFile fire
-- against a pane that is gone.
invariant EditorFileAccompaniesEditorPane {
    for p in AgentTreePanes:
        (p.editor_pane = null) = (p.editor_file = null)
}

-- A pane is always rooted at its task's worktree — never at the parent repo,
-- and never at another task's worktree. This is what the real-tmux harness
-- observes directly: a pane whose resolved cwd is the parent repo is the #231
-- failure mode the split hook exists to prevent.
invariant TaskPaneRootIsTaskWorktree {
    for p in AgentTreePanes:
        p.root = p.task.worktree?.path
}

-- Modified beats Read: no node may show a Read badge while a modifying event
-- exists for it.
invariant ModifiedBadgeWinsOverRead {
    for n in AgentTreeNodes:
        n.badge = read implies not events_for_node(n).any(e => e.operation = modified)
}

-- Expansion is a directory-only property, and it follows touched descendants
-- exactly: no expanded file nodes, no expanded-but-untouched directories, no
-- collapsed directory hiding a badge on first render.
invariant DirectoryExpansionFollowsTouchedDescendants {
    for n in AgentTreeNodes:
        (n.expanded implies n.kind = directory)
        and (n.kind = directory implies n.expanded = has_touched_descendant_node(n))
}

-- Files are never badged as directories are: a directory carries no badge, so a
-- badge on a node always means "this exact file was touched".
invariant OnlyFilesCarryBadges {
    for n in AgentTreeNodes:
        n.kind = directory implies n.badge = null
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface AgentTreeCompanionPane {
    facing user: User

    -- One surface instance per companion pane. The user reading it is looking
    -- at the agent's own tmux window, not at the kanban board.
    context pane: AgentTreePane

    exposes:
        pane.status
        pane.root
        pane.task
        pane.editor_file
        pane.error_notice

    provides:
        -- The global tmux binding (prefix + config.agent_tree_toggle_key).
        -- Acts on the pane belonging to the currently focused window. See
        -- HideAgentTreePane / ShowAgentTreePane / BindAgentTreeToggle for the
        -- implementation.
        ToggleAgentTreePane(user)

        -- Keys handled by the renderer inside the pane itself, unprefixed,
        -- while the pane has tmux focus. Every one but the exit key acts on
        -- the view only. Each cursor and expansion action carries a vim motion
        -- and an arrow key bound to the same action; the two are exact aliases,
        -- so nothing distinguishes which one the user pressed. Toggle is the
        -- exception, keyed by Space and Enter.
        ExitAgentTreeRenderer(user, pane)              -- q, Ctrl-C
        MoveAgentTreeCursorUp(user, pane)              -- k, Up
        MoveAgentTreeCursorDown(user, pane)            -- j, Down

        -- Collapse doubles as "step out": when the selected node is already
        -- closed (or is a file), the cursor moves to its parent instead.
        CollapseSelectedAgentTreeNode(user, pane)      -- h, Left

        -- Expand acts on directories only. On a file node it is a no-op: a
        -- directory is the only thing with something to open, so the pane's
        -- expansion state is left untouched and a following collapse steps out
        -- to the parent on the first press rather than the second.
        ExpandSelectedAgentTreeNode(user, pane)        -- l, Right

        -- Space and Enter dispatch on the selected node's kind, so the two
        -- actions below never contend for them: a directory toggles, a file
        -- opens. Toggle is therefore directory-only in the same way expand is.
        ToggleSelectedAgentTreeNode(user, pane)        -- Space, Enter (directory)
        OpenSelectedAgentTreeFile(user, pane, node)    -- Space, Enter (file)

    @guarantee ReadOnlyObservation
        -- The pane only reads the task's event log. It never writes to the
        -- worktree, never mutates the task, and never issues an MCP call.
        -- Killing it, or never opening it, cannot change what the agent does or
        -- what the board shows.
        --
        -- OpenSelectedAgentTreeFile does not breach this, and the distinction is
        -- worth stating precisely: the renderer launches an editor process in a
        -- pane beside itself, at the user's explicit keypress. The renderer
        -- writes nothing; the editor it hands the file to is the user's, and
        -- what they do with it is theirs. What this guarantee still forbids —
        -- the pane modifying the worktree, the task or the board of its own
        -- accord — is unchanged.

    @guarantee NoBoardCoupling
        -- The pane is a separate process in a separate window from the board
        -- TUI. It does not appear in the board's App state, does not
        -- participate in the board's DB refresh cycle, and introduces no board
        -- input mode. The board's only involvement is owning the lifetime of
        -- the toggle keybinding.

    @guidance
        -- The pane's initial state is shown, so an agent window opens with the
        -- tree already visible. Everything the pane displays is derived from
        -- the event log, so it survives its own restart: toggling off and on
        -- rebuilds the same badges.
        --
        -- The cursor position and the user's manual expansions do NOT survive a
        -- restart. They live in the renderer process and are modelled nowhere in
        -- this spec — see the AgentTreeViewState open question. What does survive
        -- a refresh is the manual expansion state within one renderer's life: a
        -- directory the user collapsed stays collapsed when new events land.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "AgentTreeViewState: the renderer's cursor position and the set of directories the user has manually expanded or collapsed are real, observable state — CollapseSelectedAgentTreeNode and friends act on them, and DirectoryExpansionFollowsTouchedDescendants only constrains the first render, not what the user does afterwards. They are currently modelled nowhere. Should AgentTreePane carry them as fields (making the manual-override behaviour rule-expressible and the invariant honest about when it applies), or is per-process view state deliberately below this spec's abstraction line?"

open question "EventLogRetention: <dispatch_data_dir>/<file_events_subdir>/<task_id>.jsonl grows unbounded for the life of a task. A long agent session touching the same files repeatedly writes one line per touch, and nothing truncates, rotates or caps the file — the same gap observability.allium records for trajectory files. Should there be a size cap, a dedup-on-write, or a wrap-up-time truncation?"

open question "OutOfWorktreeTouches: events whose path lies outside the pane root are captured but contribute no node, so an agent reading a global config file or a sibling repo leaves no visible trace. Should those touches be surfaced somewhere in the pane (a flat outside-worktree section) or is silently dropping them from the tree the intended behaviour?"
